WEBVTT

00:07.623 --> 00:14.906
[SPEAKER_00]: Yo yo yo, what is up pilots across the world, welcome back to your weekly dose of awesomeness here at paragliding atlas. In case you're new here,

00:15.067 --> 00:21.370
[SPEAKER_00]: I'm your host Anindar Singh and in case you haven't done so already, go do me a favour and smash that like and subscribe button and drop me a rating

00:21.390 --> 00:29.113
[SPEAKER_00]: wherever possible because simply that is the most efficient way we can force the algorithms to bring this awesomeness of all things

00:29.173 --> 00:37.815
[SPEAKER_00]: paragliding to more pilots out there and eventually make the skies a better place to be. And as you might have noticed, we are releasing this

00:37.915 --> 00:46.723
[SPEAKER_00]: episode a bit ahead of schedule. And the reason for that is we have so much amazing content lined up and coming your way that we simply do not have

00:46.763 --> 00:55.812
[SPEAKER_00]: time to stagger it out for later releases. But... With that being said, the only catch is that we have enough internet to actually make it happen

00:55.872 --> 01:03.577
[SPEAKER_00]: because we are traveling to some really amazing places on the planet Earth. And I'm not going to spill all the beans here. So as I always say,

01:04.117 --> 01:13.700
[SPEAKER_00]: stay tuned, stay excited because the magic is just getting started. Anyways, as amazing as it sounds, only time will tell how much of that we

01:13.740 --> 01:20.403
[SPEAKER_00]: are able to manage. But for now, let's focus on today's episode. And guess what, ladies and gentlemen, boys and girls and everyone along

01:20.443 --> 01:31.249
[SPEAKER_00]: with them, it's once again time to bring back the man, the myth, and one of the very own of the OG tribe that were the first ones who more or less

01:31.349 --> 01:41.532
[SPEAKER_00]: invented the art of what we know today as Volbev adventuring. And this time around, he's sharing much more than just navigating the skies. And

01:41.572 --> 01:48.818
[SPEAKER_00]: with that, I mean that he's sharing some of the most amazing stories and memories that he has managed to create with over three decades of flying

01:49.139 --> 01:57.887
[SPEAKER_00]: while exploring the world from above, hanging under a humble aircraft called paraglider. And that too, with some of the most pioneering

01:57.947 --> 02:05.271
[SPEAKER_00]: pilots that our sport has seen from back in the day. But yeah, enough of hyping this up for now. I would say you guys are smart enough to know where

02:05.291 --> 02:11.673
[SPEAKER_00]: to put in your effort and find out what I'm talking about. So for now, let's dive right in and I hope you like it.

02:18.394 --> 02:26.776
[SPEAKER_00]: Eddie Colfox, what an absolute honor to see you second time on the show, sir. Welcome to Paragliding Atlas one more time. It's a pleasure to be

02:26.836 --> 02:38.697
[SPEAKER_01]: here again. It's nice to talk to you again, Aninda. That was my luck. I'm just older than you. Older, wiser, and more experienced, I think. Let me

02:38.757 --> 02:49.400
[SPEAKER_00]: add on to that one. Fair enough, Eddie. This time around, we are joining in for a very special topic, which is the topic of storytellers. And I

02:49.440 --> 02:59.323
[SPEAKER_00]: believe somebody with your experience who has seen the sport... evolve and thrive and survive through various dynamics during the past years.

03:00.463 --> 03:12.767
[SPEAKER_00]: Tell us a little bit about what our audiences are in for and what does three decades of experience bring in from your end today? I was born in

03:12.827 --> 03:24.124
[SPEAKER_01]: 1969, makes me 56 years old now, and I just loved the idea of flying. I was massively influenced by all the hang gliding on TV in the 70s. when it

03:24.164 --> 03:34.959
[SPEAKER_01]: first came out and then I had a cousin that did it and I was just determined to do it as soon as I could. But that took a while because I first viewed it

03:34.999 --> 03:45.012
[SPEAKER_01]: when I was like 15 or 13. And then I had a cousin that died hang gliding, so my family weren't keen on letting me do it for a little while. I had to wait

03:45.092 --> 03:55.400
[SPEAKER_01]: until I was independently able to get to places. Sorry for your loss, Eddie. It's quite a tricky thing to navigate in this sport. It wasn't

03:55.420 --> 04:01.565
[SPEAKER_01]: good. That's the wrong word to use. But yeah, it was an early introduction to the fact, although it looked really exciting and fun,

04:01.785 --> 04:11.980
[SPEAKER_01]: that it was very real and there was danger. Fair enough, Eddie. But just for the perspective of audiences, I think the write-up that you shared,

04:12.220 --> 04:22.411
[SPEAKER_00]: you had quite a detailed description of Ross Somerville and your first paragliding flight. Let's dive right into it. How did the art of flight

04:22.491 --> 04:31.979
[SPEAKER_00]: entice you and how did you get into this? My first paragliding flight was after... three years of wanting to learn to hang glide at the time i was

04:32.059 --> 04:38.802
[SPEAKER_01]: working on ships and it was difficult to get it together because i couldn't carry it around and the schools were all in different parts of

04:38.822 --> 04:46.846
[SPEAKER_01]: the country and i was hardly ever on leave i was hardly ever in the uk but but in so doing with the hang gliding i'd met this guy ross he was a good

04:46.886 --> 04:56.094
[SPEAKER_01]: mate of a good mate and one of the very early proponents of paragliding. I wasn't there at its inception. I started in 92, I think, 91, 92.

04:58.516 --> 05:08.725
[SPEAKER_01]: But Ross had something called a hot dream. He'd had a hot dream flight where he didn't accidentally engage with Seabreeze Convergence. He'd

05:08.745 --> 05:21.204
[SPEAKER_01]: done a 98-kilometer flight way back in 1989 or 1990, I believe. I have to check the stats somewhere. And then, so I was in the UK and Ross came to

05:21.244 --> 05:30.488
[SPEAKER_01]: visit and we went for a walk. He insisted on bringing this strange nylon bag with him, this little bag. And he insisted on walking up a particular

05:30.528 --> 05:38.712
[SPEAKER_01]: hill. I thought that was normal because he was a hang glider pilot and I was a keen but hardly flowing hang glider pilot. And we wandered up this

05:38.752 --> 05:49.512
[SPEAKER_01]: hill coincidentally, of course, was facing into wind. And then he started to get this. He let me in on the truck and said, this is a

05:49.572 --> 05:56.314
[SPEAKER_01]: paraglider and this is what I've achieved. We had a lot of fun being dragged through cowpats on top of the hill, ground handling it for a bit.

05:57.034 --> 06:05.117
[SPEAKER_01]: It was quite windy because we were getting dragged through cowpats. And then he bummed me off the hill. That was my first flight. I flew in a

06:05.157 --> 06:14.025
[SPEAKER_01]: straight line for quite a long time and landed. It was a big adventure, but I wasn't completely green. I'd done a little bit of hang gliding and

06:14.526 --> 06:23.410
[SPEAKER_01]: we did spend 10, 15 minutes being dragged through cowpats before I got bummed off the hill. Thank you for your honesty, Eddie. A lot of us go

06:23.450 --> 06:30.474
[SPEAKER_00]: through our own adventures when we learn to ground handle. But Eddie, just for perspective, how did the free flight scenario look at that

06:30.534 --> 06:38.638
[SPEAKER_00]: time? Was hang gliding ruling the scene up to a point where it was easy enough to someone to just be curious about it and there were providers

06:38.698 --> 06:47.182
[SPEAKER_00]: out there? Or did people have to really dig in and find the right person and get in the community and get the trust? What was it like if you wanted

06:47.202 --> 06:55.588
[SPEAKER_00]: to fly back in the day? You had to dig in and go and give it a go. You had to be prepared to get in the car and drive to places. But then there were

06:55.628 --> 07:01.932
[SPEAKER_01]: schools in the UK. There were many more hang gliding schools than there currently are, but there still weren't very many. There was one in

07:01.992 --> 07:11.418
[SPEAKER_01]: Dartmoor. And in my early days of paragliding, I had to go all the way to Derbyshire or Wales to get it together. But I was very fortunate in that

07:11.638 --> 07:22.133
[SPEAKER_01]: within a year of trying paragliding, a chap called Andrew Pierce, who's a great mate, There was also a school in the Isle of Wight, High

07:22.153 --> 07:29.415
[SPEAKER_01]: Adventure, and Andrew had been working for High Adventure, which was part of Airwave, which was the precursor of Ozone, and half the team of

07:29.535 --> 07:39.797
[SPEAKER_01]: Airwave ended up starting up Ozone. And Andrew was part of that crowd, and he came and started a school, Flying Frenzy, and I was its first

07:39.837 --> 07:48.276
[SPEAKER_01]: student, although I'd done it a little bit prior to meeting him. And Andrew and I, we're both farmer's sons from this part of the country,

07:48.536 --> 07:58.382
[SPEAKER_01]: West Dorset on the south coast of the UK. And under Andrew's guidance, Andrew set up Flying Frenzy as a student and a co-farmer. We managed to

07:58.402 --> 08:07.207
[SPEAKER_01]: get some permissions locally, and it was great. He took me through my first year or so of properly learning to fly. I scared him witless

08:07.227 --> 08:16.030
[SPEAKER_01]: because I was full of young dumb and full of cum or whatever, however they said in the UK. I was very enthusiastic. I got picked out of many bushes

08:16.110 --> 08:26.539
[SPEAKER_01]: and small trees. He kept me safe and taught me a huge amount. He and I have had a long flying relationship, which we still do. I ended up being

08:26.579 --> 08:34.906
[SPEAKER_01]: involved in running the school and working. I became an instructor very early on in my paragliding. Of course, one needs to work to be able to

08:35.146 --> 08:41.591
[SPEAKER_01]: spend lots of time doing something. You either need to find the money and leisure time or you live the job. And that's what I chose to do. I became a

08:41.631 --> 08:49.320
[SPEAKER_01]: paragliding bum. I did a little bit of instruction and all the British comps and met some wonderful people. That's when I first met John

08:49.360 --> 09:00.564
[SPEAKER_01]: Sylvester, who I imagine 90% of people on this podcast will know of him, if not look him up, because he was the first very visible Volbiv pilot.

09:00.644 --> 09:09.967
[SPEAKER_01]: There's a fantastic film from nowhere to the middle of nowhere that was made in the first year I went to India, I believe. I think it was made in

09:11.525 --> 09:23.329
[SPEAKER_01]: 1994. four or five which where he and this guy alan hughes did this massive flight from dharamsala to most of the way to nepal not one flight

09:23.349 --> 09:30.631
[SPEAKER_01]: of course and a number of flights camping out they did it on on tandem and so in my opinion it's the best paragliding movie of all time because it

09:30.731 --> 09:39.896
[SPEAKER_01]: shows the general public what it's like because alan hughes's face just reflects all the fear and excitement of all your first flights, but he's

09:39.916 --> 09:48.657
[SPEAKER_01]: doing this massive vol-biv across a lot of the Himalayan range. I've seen that video multiple times and one thing that blows my mind is that

09:48.737 --> 09:57.879
[SPEAKER_00]: back in the day when GoPros and action cameras were a distant dream, just figuring out how to stay in the air longer than planned or finding the

09:58.159 --> 10:06.221
[SPEAKER_00]: appropriate clothing and the safety mechanism along with it were quite a challenge. And then these guys go out there with the camera record all

10:06.241 --> 10:13.848
[SPEAKER_00]: of that and edit it and put it out in one of the most enriching storytelling manners I've seen. So I think we'll put a link to that video

10:13.928 --> 10:20.714
[SPEAKER_00]: in the show notes. And also the Birdman and the Karakoram one, which was a later one that I was involved in, that was still using fairly big

10:20.774 --> 10:29.116
[SPEAKER_01]: cameras. I made this extraordinary one. It was really just a fun thing because the first time I went to Hunza in Pakistan, I got given this

10:29.357 --> 10:38.462
[SPEAKER_01]: massive camera that I just couldn't put anywhere because it was so big. I couldn't attach it up in the lines. It would have killed me. And so I ended

10:38.542 --> 10:47.265
[SPEAKER_01]: up just having it between my legs. And consequently, it got a dirty lens and everything. But it was just, as you say, extraordinary to be able to

10:47.385 --> 10:55.771
[SPEAKER_01]: use that tech and those sorts of demanding conditions. But Alan had come from a kayaking and climbing filmmaking background. He'd made some

10:55.831 --> 11:04.777
[SPEAKER_01]: amazing films. Stone Monkey is a fantastic film that he made. And I can't remember the names of the other ones, but look him up. Alan, A-L-U-N

11:04.897 --> 11:13.980
[SPEAKER_01]: Hughes, H-U-G-H-E-S Hughes. There's some extraordinary stuff that he made. He's an old man now. He's in his 70s. So yeah, people have been doing

11:14.080 --> 11:22.106
[SPEAKER_01]: amazing things for ages. Absolutely. And that is the reason why a pioneer pilot like you and everybody else that you shared the airspace

11:22.126 --> 11:30.272
[SPEAKER_00]: with can inspire the next generation with your stories. But take me back in time, Eddie. How was the visualization like? How did you see that the

11:30.292 --> 11:38.539
[SPEAKER_00]: sport would grow? Because let's say when you were just getting the early hours under the wing, when cross-country wasn't even a thing invented

11:38.779 --> 11:47.287
[SPEAKER_00]: or something that people would plan out, what was the definition of having fun with a paraglider look like? It's not true to say no one had

11:47.387 --> 11:54.149
[SPEAKER_01]: gone cross-country, because as I just said, Ross had done this amazing flight, and people had been doing small flights. Ross held that record

11:54.189 --> 12:03.891
[SPEAKER_01]: for ages, because it was a lucky break that he went quite so far. People were doing 20, 30, 50-kilometre flights in the UK at that time, and

12:03.971 --> 12:11.470
[SPEAKER_01]: overseas it was greater. I can't remember what the South... There was a South African... No, Namibian record. At that time, that was the world

12:11.550 --> 12:20.873
[SPEAKER_01]: record. And I can't remember the numbers. I'm sorry. But it was over 100. It was quite a long way. It was possibly over 200 even then. And what kind

12:20.893 --> 12:30.776
[SPEAKER_01]: of glide ratios were the wings? The top wings, I'm trying to remember what the top wing was. I think it was about seven, six and a half. Training

12:30.837 --> 12:40.995
[SPEAKER_01]: wings were less than that. By about 95, you were getting things like the Nova Xenon. I may be wrong with my numbers, but some proper tech head will

12:41.015 --> 12:48.638
[SPEAKER_01]: get in touch with you and say, Eddie got it slightly wrong. Roughly the early to mid 90s, the Nova Xenon came out and that was definitely a game

12:48.678 --> 12:58.883
[SPEAKER_01]: changer in my early years. But just before, just as I was around, there was the Airwave Black Magic. That was just as I arrived in the sport. And

12:59.023 --> 13:08.105
[SPEAKER_01]: there were some groundbreaking flights. The world record out in return was set in beer. by a French guy called Xavier Raymond in a competition

13:08.165 --> 13:17.488
[SPEAKER_01]: that I think was 1989. Fair enough. Going out and chasing adventure, was it basically about, like, were people actually taking all of these

13:17.848 --> 13:24.991
[SPEAKER_00]: pilgrimages or travels specifically for cross-country, or was it more or less that they were still experimenting with the equipment and

13:25.031 --> 13:34.415
[SPEAKER_00]: figuring out how to get the know-how? Cross-country was an aspiration of only a few members of the club. I think is the way to answer this. Most

13:34.475 --> 13:42.961
[SPEAKER_01]: people were very content just to soar up and down on whichever ridge they were on. But people were definitely going cross-country, but it was

13:43.061 --> 13:52.648
[SPEAKER_01]: only a handful in the club. Nowadays, of course, at least half the British clubs are expecting to go cross-country. The people now in

13:52.688 --> 14:01.324
[SPEAKER_01]: their first couple of years, the ones that are as keen as I was, are probably doing 100k flights. The equipment has definitely vastly

14:01.384 --> 14:12.008
[SPEAKER_01]: improved. And it's safer. That's the thing. The comp wing back then was really scary. You'd expect the comps that I was doing back then, you'd

14:12.068 --> 14:20.831
[SPEAKER_01]: expect to have massive collapses. You'd expect to be turned on every good day. You'd go chasing. If you were chasing the lead gaggle or part of

14:20.851 --> 14:28.302
[SPEAKER_01]: the lead gaggle, you'd see someone go into a thermal. You'd see half their wing fold up. You'd see them correct it. And that's where you'd go.

14:28.502 --> 14:34.824
[SPEAKER_01]: That's where the good stuff was. Love it. The mayhem, as we all know, cloud-based mayhem.

14:37.426 --> 14:44.149
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, I'm so glad that there were like a bunch of people who believed in this madness and still made the most out of it, regardless of how many

14:44.169 --> 14:51.212
[SPEAKER_00]: challenges it came. But how did the acro scene look like, Eddie? Was it just the wing-overs or were people doing a bit more than that? Or were

14:51.252 --> 14:56.686
[SPEAKER_00]: there anything at all? It was... I think on purpose, just the wing-overs.

14:59.728 --> 15:08.494
[SPEAKER_00]: Fair enough. The thing being on purpose. Alright, because acro came in fairly later on in the industry, but spiral dives, wing-overs, these

15:08.534 --> 15:16.480
[SPEAKER_00]: have been two of the basic maneuvers which have stayed since the inception of paraglider. Yeah, that's correct. Spiral dives and

15:16.500 --> 15:25.904
[SPEAKER_01]: wing-overs, we would do those. And then the beeline stall was quite common, and various other maneuvers to descend. But giving names to all

15:25.944 --> 15:35.429
[SPEAKER_01]: the Acro maneuvers was definitely not a rant. You weren't doing them in control. I'm quite glad that Acro wasn't finessed in my early days,

15:35.449 --> 15:42.012
[SPEAKER_01]: because I might have tried too hard in that scene.

15:42.092 --> 15:49.053
[SPEAKER_00]: I'm sure a lot of pilots in the audience can resonate, because everybody loves pushing their limits when they're... I don't know, is this

15:49.133 --> 15:56.876
[SPEAKER_00]: testosterone thing or is it like just whatever you said, young, dumb and whatever thing, you know, that people just love doing something out of

15:56.896 --> 16:04.279
[SPEAKER_00]: their comfort zone. It resonates with the saying that there are bold pilots and old pilots, but no bold and old pilots, which doesn't make

16:04.319 --> 16:15.144
[SPEAKER_00]: complete sense to me. But why do people slow down? Is it just the age or nature or is it actually something more to it? I think this is a deep

16:15.184 --> 16:24.604
[SPEAKER_00]: question that I've just asked you. That's a tricky question because I'm not a psychologist, and I'm not sure that we all slow down as such. I've

16:24.664 --> 16:33.425
[SPEAKER_01]: always, not always, that's not true. I had one proper accident in paragliding. I've landed in trees and things like that, but it's not

16:33.486 --> 16:42.107
[SPEAKER_01]: true to say I've had one proper one. I've had one where I've hurt myself significantly, although it was only a day or two in hospital, and it's

16:42.147 --> 16:51.411
[SPEAKER_01]: not been life-offering, so I'm very lucky. And that was entirely my fault. It was in my first two or three years of flying when I was doing

16:51.791 --> 16:59.218
[SPEAKER_01]: really well. I was winning the National League at the time. I was properly competitive. I couldn't understand why all these older hang

16:59.238 --> 17:06.805
[SPEAKER_01]: glider pilots, in my opinion at the time, were miserable. They were killjoys. They were always saying, oh, it's too strong, it's too this,

17:06.886 --> 17:17.007
[SPEAKER_01]: it's too the other. And I'd had a crazy day doing crazy stuff in land. It was very unstable. I don't really want to repeat it. I don't mind

17:17.047 --> 17:24.009
[SPEAKER_01]: repeating it online if other people learn from it, because I think it's really important to be brave and bold and say where you messed up. So I

17:24.049 --> 17:32.931
[SPEAKER_01]: messed up. I was arrogant as hell. I launched in. There were QNIM flags. I didn't get sucked up really high in one, but I'd got in very severely

17:32.991 --> 17:43.733
[SPEAKER_01]: rained on. I was really rough. Anyway, and I managed to land in a sort of small gas front. So I'd had a very adventurous day, and I'd bundled all my

17:43.933 --> 17:50.535
[SPEAKER_01]: wet gear into the back of my car, trying to dry it out. And I thought, hang on, it's sea breezing, because it was that kind of unstable day on the

17:50.575 --> 17:59.819
[SPEAKER_01]: south coast of the UK where it sea breezes. And I thought, great, I can go to the coast and I can dry my wing out and just soar around with a wet wing,

17:59.879 --> 18:08.227
[SPEAKER_01]: which isn't, of course, advised. That was my thinking. and i got to the coast and the first thing to the launch and it was gusting but it didn't

18:08.247 --> 18:18.710
[SPEAKER_01]: seem too windy i was really excited i hardly assessed the weather and i just bunged my wing out set up set up in seconds this guy i knew quite like

18:18.770 --> 18:29.059
[SPEAKER_01]: but i always didn't hang on you're a killjoy old hangy play a pilot chap came up eddie it's too windy and i ignored him and i Pulled the glider up

18:29.119 --> 18:36.804
[SPEAKER_01]: and as I turned around, there was a gas front. There was a school line in the water. And I didn't bring the glider down. I got lifted up and then I

18:36.824 --> 18:44.810
[SPEAKER_01]: got a massive caps on launch and managed to stop the glider turning severely. But I basically stalled it in from not terribly high onto a

18:44.850 --> 18:55.040
[SPEAKER_01]: slope and compressed some vertebrae. And so there you go. Your question is, why do people lose weight? Why do people become less reckless as they

18:55.080 --> 19:04.566
[SPEAKER_01]: go older? There are no old and bold pilots because I'm an old and bold pilot, but I'm less bold than I used to be. I thought I knew it all back

19:04.646 --> 19:13.732
[SPEAKER_01]: then, and I crashed. I've been flying for over 30 years, and I don't know it all. I'm learning all the time. I learn off pilots with huge

19:13.812 --> 19:22.664
[SPEAKER_01]: experience, more experience than myself, and I learn off pilots who are just starting out. A lot of it is psychology, and a lot of it's skill. But

19:22.764 --> 19:28.790
[SPEAKER_01]: your skill is useless if you have bad psychology, because it's the psychology that will kill you. It's always the fault of the human,

19:28.830 --> 19:36.617
[SPEAKER_01]: really. There's always a decision. There's a bad decision that puts you in danger, and then there's usually two or three more bad decisions that

19:36.637 --> 19:44.099
[SPEAKER_01]: you make, and then you eventually hurt yourself. You've got to learn to make the good decisions. Golden words, Eddie. I love the way you put it in

19:44.119 --> 19:54.043
[SPEAKER_00]: the last sentence that life will give you a few chances before finally getting angrier at you. And I think somebody told me this, that every

19:54.083 --> 20:03.266
[SPEAKER_00]: time the cost of lesson learned becomes heavier, the price that you pay becomes heavier and you have to learn when to finally be real to yourself

20:03.466 --> 20:10.891
[SPEAKER_00]: and make sure that you know when to push back instead of just keeping on. That's an interesting thing, which was I deliberately withdrew from

20:10.931 --> 20:19.160
[SPEAKER_01]: comps at one stage because I didn't like being told what to do. I didn't want to fly a task on some of the days. I didn't want to fly that particular

20:19.200 --> 20:29.190
[SPEAKER_01]: task. I thought it was scary and I'm competitive. I like to win. I like to do well. And I got scared doing the wrong. I don't blame the task setters.

20:29.250 --> 20:35.881
[SPEAKER_01]: It's impossible. a really difficult job. So occasionally the task setters would choose a task and the weather would be wrong for that

20:35.961 --> 20:44.226
[SPEAKER_01]: particular day and you'd end up... Anyway, it was really scary occasionally. Yeah, the other thing is that I've become slightly less

20:44.287 --> 20:51.231
[SPEAKER_01]: of an adventure pilot than I used to be. We were the first people to fly in Hunza and things like that. I did some 180km out and returned there in

20:51.291 --> 20:52.913
[SPEAKER_01]: 2004 and did some 7,800m heights and things like that.

21:00.126 --> 21:13.978
[SPEAKER_01]: And I'm not that interested in doing that kind of thing so much anymore. Or I am, I love it. But the guiding and the tandems are a great way. It is an

21:14.058 --> 21:23.425
[SPEAKER_01]: adrenaline sport and to get the same adrenaline, you have to keep pushing your personal edge. I've been a bit, I'm not that keen on doing

21:23.465 --> 21:37.398
[SPEAKER_01]: that anymore because I'm mortal, I'm very mortal and I'm very much in love with my life, the things I do and the people that are in it. So I'm keen

21:37.438 --> 21:44.861
[SPEAKER_01]: to do those adventures, but I won't push it to the same degree. I'll fly away from a top landing or I'll fly away from a cold because I'm too

21:44.901 --> 21:52.064
[SPEAKER_01]: scared. When I started, I had a great degree of ignorance. I didn't see the risks that are over the corner. Now I know more about the risks that

21:52.084 --> 22:02.271
[SPEAKER_01]: are around the corner. And I'm just more cautious with it. I've discovered a way to enjoy it doing guiding and tandems. Usually I'm

22:02.311 --> 22:10.816
[SPEAKER_01]: guiding people with considerably less experience than me when flying tandem pilots. And usually it's our first flight. And each time I

22:10.856 --> 22:19.642
[SPEAKER_01]: parasitically or vicariously experience their thrill. So I'm throwing myself back to being a less experienced pilot and gaining that

22:19.742 --> 22:30.097
[SPEAKER_01]: buzz that they're having. It's great. I think that's a great way for an instructor or a guide to put themselves in where you try and look at the

22:30.137 --> 22:40.664
[SPEAKER_00]: task from your client's eyes, which are less experienced, as you said, and then you basically find the ways to make it fun within their limits.

22:41.845 --> 22:48.789
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, I didn't make that very clear. Thank you very much for editing that in that way. Obviously, you don't want to scare the wits out of your

22:48.809 --> 22:58.944
[SPEAKER_01]: customer or your client. But you can have a great parasitic, vicarious thrill by doing something well within your own limits and within

22:58.984 --> 23:07.634
[SPEAKER_01]: theirs. Just out of curiosity, Eddie, how many hours do you have in total? I have less than some people and more than most. I'm sorry to be

23:07.674 --> 23:15.798
[SPEAKER_01]: vague about that. I'm not that great at all my record keeping. But I don't know the exact answer, but probably 6,000 hours. I'm slightly

23:15.858 --> 23:23.004
[SPEAKER_01]: reluctant to put that out there. But if you've been flying for 30 odd years, and let's say just over that time, you've averaged well over 100

23:23.144 --> 23:32.892
[SPEAKER_01]: hours. I've got at least 3,000 on that basis. And many of those years I've flown three or 400 hours. Fair enough. I don't want to put you in a spot

23:32.932 --> 23:36.735
[SPEAKER_00]: here. So I'm doing a guesstimate. I've got somewhere between 4,000 and 7,000 hours.

23:40.033 --> 23:47.898
[SPEAKER_00]: What blows me about the numbers, thousands, is that in commercial aviation, thousands of hours come fairly quickly. And the effort

23:47.918 --> 23:56.143
[SPEAKER_00]: required is very different. You just sit in a cockpit, you have a very set flight plan, and then you just rake up hours, like 10 hours every flight

23:56.203 --> 24:03.128
[SPEAKER_00]: or five hours every flight or something. If you treat it as a work, you pretty much rake up hours in three, four years. But in paragliding, on

24:03.168 --> 24:11.711
[SPEAKER_00]: the other hand, hours is a whole different game. Yeah. And back in the day, I've got my first log book, and my first log book has got loads of

24:11.751 --> 24:21.359
[SPEAKER_01]: flights of one minute in it. It's got a few of 15, 20, 30 minutes, and that's coastal soaring flights. And that's the difference. Nowadays,

24:21.639 --> 24:33.189
[SPEAKER_01]: I don't know if you can, if you live in the right country, you can get 150 hours in your first year. Anyone with over 100 hours back in the 90s was a

24:33.249 --> 24:44.413
[SPEAKER_01]: rock star. Wow. Help us understand a little bit about the manufacturing side of it, Eddie. The brands and the equipment back in the day, because

24:44.573 --> 24:52.475
[SPEAKER_00]: hang gliding was an established industry where people were ready to invest money and it made sense for the manufacturers. But paragliding,

24:52.495 --> 25:00.377
[SPEAKER_00]: of course, there were only few pioneer pilots like you and your tribe who were going out and chasing big adventures with these massive cameras.

25:00.957 --> 25:11.320
[SPEAKER_00]: How was the availability of trustworthy gear? And how was the support from the gear manufacturers for pilots like you who were out there

25:11.520 --> 25:21.523
[SPEAKER_00]: making the most out of it and spreading the good word? In the properly early days, just before, you could get wings off manufacturers for

25:21.603 --> 25:31.886
[SPEAKER_01]: trips relatively easily, and they were massively supportive. Companies like Nova were very generous with our first Hunza trips.

25:32.832 --> 25:40.102
[SPEAKER_01]: Manufacturers are keen to help if you've got a good story, but I think it's harder nowadays to get good assistance. I did get some

25:40.122 --> 25:48.173
[SPEAKER_01]: sponsorship. Firebird were very generous to me. Nova were very generous to me. Gin were very generous to me. they were very supportive

25:48.273 --> 25:57.001
[SPEAKER_01]: they still are and there's so many it's so much easier i think nowadays to get really big exposure with all the filmmaking abilities and the

25:57.061 --> 26:05.340
[SPEAKER_01]: internet with the social network there's a lot you can give back to a manufacturer you've got to be able to give something back So back in the

26:05.380 --> 26:16.983
[SPEAKER_00]: day when you guys were chasing all the adventures, what was the usual trick to give back to the companies in return? To give back was stories in

26:17.023 --> 26:28.624
[SPEAKER_01]: magazines, winning competitions, gaining records. Those were the main ways. You had to be super organized to do that. It had to be well

26:28.684 --> 26:35.956
[SPEAKER_01]: written as well. I wasn't too great at that kind of thing. I was an adventurer. If you were super good, there were many more professional

26:36.577 --> 26:44.674
[SPEAKER_01]: pilots employed by manufacturers as opposed to guiding or teaching. I'll tell you the reason why I'm asking this question. That is because

26:45.134 --> 26:56.756
[SPEAKER_00]: often we have seen camera is a bit of a safety hazard and people are ready to push the safety limits to get the perfect shot. Today it's a GoPro or

26:56.776 --> 27:06.078
[SPEAKER_00]: Insta360 which is on a stick and people like to put out the most amazing content or the most viral worthy content. Back in the days, you guys were

27:06.098 --> 27:15.720
[SPEAKER_00]: traveling with cameras, which were as big as the size of a laptop, which was, again, a safety hazard. So my curiosity stemmed from the fact that

27:15.900 --> 27:24.961
[SPEAKER_00]: has the way of taking risk changed over the years when it comes to creating value for somebody who is paying you for it? Or has it been

27:25.101 --> 27:33.683
[SPEAKER_00]: pretty much the same way people have navigated this, but just that goods have changed and toys have changed, but the human psyche is still the

27:33.723 --> 27:43.663
[SPEAKER_00]: same around it? I think we've got to be real about this. Paragliding attracts risk takers. We've always been risk takers. And also it

27:43.723 --> 27:52.168
[SPEAKER_01]: attracts people who either want to, it's compulsive enough that you want to make it your life and you want to make it your life. You have to seek

27:52.208 --> 28:00.210
[SPEAKER_01]: a way of funding it and funding it means making films. So you'll take those risks. Nowadays, everyone's doing it. I never have a camera. I

28:00.250 --> 28:06.214
[SPEAKER_01]: never have a little camera on my head. And I'd never put a big camera on my head, even if it was possible, because I'm from the school of

28:06.274 --> 28:15.161
[SPEAKER_01]: paragliding where our early comp wings, my early comp wings, I'd expect the wing to collapse. That might remove my helmet. I was at a British

28:15.481 --> 28:22.386
[SPEAKER_01]: paragliding competition where it came on the radio. I've had a big collapse. It ripped my helmet off, but I'm still flying around. I'm

28:22.446 --> 28:33.173
[SPEAKER_01]: trying to get this turn point. This was Guy Anderson. It came on a radio. The caps were taking people's helmets off back then. Did it not snap

28:33.293 --> 28:41.335
[SPEAKER_00]: people's necks? That is the first thing that comes to mind. If it catches a line of your camera, it's straight away going to impact on your neck

28:41.455 --> 28:50.596
[SPEAKER_00]: instantly as soon as it reopens. I haven't heard of that actually happening, but there have been some inexplicable accidents where

28:50.636 --> 29:00.410
[SPEAKER_01]: people have crashed and it's suspected that The lines have got caught up on a camera. I don't know whether that's actually been proven. You might

29:00.450 --> 29:08.276
[SPEAKER_01]: be able to correct me on that. But it doesn't make good sense to me to fly with a helmet cam. I wonder if there's an opportunity for someone to use

29:08.316 --> 29:17.244
[SPEAKER_01]: what those printers called, where you can print solid objects and maybe design something that went underneath the average hero cam, the

29:17.304 --> 29:25.275
[SPEAKER_01]: average GoPro or Instagram or whatever you call them. I'm not a tech head, as you can tell, but you can get those printers that make solid

29:25.335 --> 29:31.864
[SPEAKER_01]: objects. I imagine there's an opening there for someone to sell that sort of thing through social networks. While we are talking about

29:31.904 --> 29:42.952
[SPEAKER_00]: Chasing Adventures, Eddie, Hunza and Bir and all of these were, of course, pretty amazing sites. But how did the thought of sharing it with

29:43.032 --> 29:50.657
[SPEAKER_00]: others come back in the day? And if you can talk to our listeners about how did that aspect grow and develop into something so meaningful that

29:51.178 --> 29:58.543
[SPEAKER_00]: eventually the whole camaraderie between you and everyone else involved was able to be spread over to a larger audience to whosoever was

29:58.583 --> 30:08.410
[SPEAKER_00]: willing to be a part of it? I was lucky in some respects. I was focusing on being an adventure pilot up until about 2006 or so. I'd done lots of trips

30:08.451 --> 30:17.755
[SPEAKER_01]: to Pakistan, lots of trips to India. I'd been working in Pune. I'd met this wonderful guy called Richard Jones. He'd been an adventure

30:17.795 --> 30:27.099
[SPEAKER_01]: businessman in paragliding in India. He'd been teaching the Pune ashram paragliding and he'd been doing lots of stuff in Goa, getting

30:27.139 --> 30:39.881
[SPEAKER_01]: people high on a line. He'd had a tow syndicate in South Anjuna. He turned up one day when I was in Wales and, um, And he turned up on the hill and he

30:39.921 --> 30:47.704
[SPEAKER_01]: wanted to buy a load of tandems. And then he also said, do you want a job out there? Come and join me. And it was the end of the summer in Wales. The end

30:47.744 --> 30:54.547
[SPEAKER_01]: of the summer in Wales, if you're a paraglider pilot, is quite a depressing place to be. It's a fantastic place to fly in the summer when

30:54.567 --> 31:02.769
[SPEAKER_01]: the weather is great. It's a very wet part of the country here in the UK. And so I jumped at it. I went out there and I ended up doing two winter

31:02.809 --> 31:11.795
[SPEAKER_01]: seasons in both Pune and Goa. And that got me into India. I hadn't been very familiar with India prior to that at all. I'd never been there. I

31:11.835 --> 31:21.601
[SPEAKER_01]: knew some Indian people, but I hadn't been there. I loved both the people and the country. So I made a point of going back as often as I could. And one

31:21.621 --> 31:27.521
[SPEAKER_01]: of those times I went with John Sylvester. to Nanda Devi in 2005

31:29.042 --> 31:37.666
[SPEAKER_01]: when I was working with Brinkley. I met up with Devi Chowdhury, who's a paragliding god in both India and France and elsewhere. Fantastic guy,

31:38.346 --> 31:47.110
[SPEAKER_01]: multilingual, very generous with his knowledge and massively able as a pilot. And he was only a youngster. He was like 15, 16 then. He just

31:47.150 --> 31:57.940
[SPEAKER_01]: started out in flying. And then Bruce Mills, he's another legend from beer. He was really the first Westerner to be there and in the

31:57.980 --> 32:08.143
[SPEAKER_01]: paragliding world. And so I met those guys early on. And then in 2006, I went to Nanda Devi with John. We had a really exciting time, but it was too

32:08.183 --> 32:15.726
[SPEAKER_01]: exciting for me. We were landing in gorges and having to climb out, and I'm not a great climber. It was properly scary. John was doing some

32:15.766 --> 32:25.823
[SPEAKER_01]: fantastic climbing. very short flights. Each one of them was at huge risk to oneself, and I'd had enough of that. It wasn't what I wanted, and I

32:25.863 --> 32:32.665
[SPEAKER_01]: wasn't a great climber as well. So I was putting myself in even more risk. I couldn't climb out to these places. I didn't have the skills or

32:32.725 --> 32:42.848
[SPEAKER_01]: experience to do that. I was quite an experienced paraglider pilot, but I was a very bad climber. So I chickened out. We had a discussion. John

32:42.888 --> 32:52.031
[SPEAKER_01]: stayed on for a bit. But I got on a bus and I went to beer to meet up with my old friends. I'd only been to beer one time before that and it wasn't in the

32:52.071 --> 32:59.596
[SPEAKER_01]: flying season and I hadn't flown. And I wanted to go out there. It was a perfect time to go. John was there. Debbie was there. Bruce was there.

32:59.977 --> 33:07.402
[SPEAKER_01]: And loads of people like Mike were there. Antoine, I think, was there. And loads

33:07.462 --> 33:18.650
[SPEAKER_01]: of the old beer heads were there. It was fantastic. Gilles Monnier, who I'd originally met Dan and Pooner as well. He was there. We flew to Kulu on

33:19.070 --> 33:27.499
[SPEAKER_01]: my second day there with Jim and it was super exciting. Since then, I just kept on coming back and I met up with all these guys. I think your original

33:27.559 --> 33:38.484
[SPEAKER_01]: question is how did it develop? It developed because of beer's wonderful season it's a short season which is a pain but it's one of its

33:38.524 --> 33:45.687
[SPEAKER_01]: great things because it means everyone is there and you meet them all together everyone who loves it so and can has got a flexible enough life

33:45.887 --> 33:55.250
[SPEAKER_01]: that they can they're all there for that season and um and it's such and so it's one of the best places to fly for for people who are interested to

33:55.450 --> 34:06.572
[SPEAKER_01]: involve it it's a fantastic place apart from With respect to the Indians and the huge amount of development that they have, good development

34:06.612 --> 34:14.536
[SPEAKER_01]: that they have achieved, the only downside is you've got to be real. It's a developing country and consequently the emergent and a vast country

34:14.576 --> 34:25.201
[SPEAKER_01]: with difficult terrain. So the rescue services aren't that great. But apart from that, it's the best place that I know of to fly

34:25.521 --> 34:34.964
[SPEAKER_01]: internationally for those months. A lot of the best people are there. And they're cool. We've known each other for years. If people over a long

34:35.004 --> 34:43.392
[SPEAKER_01]: period of time, and each time you see them, you're having an adventure, then it creates that kind of camaraderie. Landing on top of a

34:43.412 --> 34:52.930
[SPEAKER_01]: mountainside and camping the night and spending it there with friends. It's heaven, in a way. It's one form of heaven. Indeed. And my curiosity

34:52.950 --> 35:02.257
[SPEAKER_00]: stems from the fact that all of you guys out there were pretty much chasing it yourself and then it was you to try and spread that. I think you

35:02.277 --> 35:11.072
[SPEAKER_00]: guys must have been the first ones to offer tours in that region, no? Yeah, I think we weren't the first commercial people out there. But I

35:11.132 --> 35:20.617
[SPEAKER_01]: think we were the first people to do genuine safaris, go and fly, spend nights on the mountain. We weren't the first people to fly to Manali,

35:20.657 --> 35:31.041
[SPEAKER_01]: although I think Debbie was. Debbie and Andy, I believe, were the first people to do that. But we made it more common. We introduced it to people.

35:31.481 --> 35:42.403
[SPEAKER_01]: Rather than it just being the great pilots, the bold, good pilots, it then became something that was achievable. by less bold but good

35:42.443 --> 35:52.629
[SPEAKER_01]: pilots. You had to be good to go. We would guide people conscientiously and try to assess people's genuine skill, maybe bolster their courage a

35:52.669 --> 36:03.472
[SPEAKER_01]: little bit by being there to assist. That was a great thing to do and we did that every year from 2006. Yeah, we've taken many people. Wow, sounds

36:03.512 --> 36:10.718
[SPEAKER_00]: like quite an adventure. But while we're talking about adventure, Eddie, John Sylvester is a name that comes up repeatedly when it comes to

36:10.758 --> 36:21.447
[SPEAKER_00]: big mountain flying. Can you tell our listeners a little bit about John? How was he like and how was he able to pioneer and push the limits and still

36:21.507 --> 36:30.074
[SPEAKER_00]: stay safe and live to tell the tale? Because there are pilots who follow his ideology in today's world as well, but he was doing it when nobody

36:30.154 --> 36:40.439
[SPEAKER_01]: else was to talk about john it's lovely because he was a very special man and a fantastic friend to me and an inspiration he was a brilliant pilot

36:40.499 --> 36:48.503
[SPEAKER_01]: that's the first thing to say he was a very dedicated person he put huge energy into the things he loved he came from a dangerous sports

36:48.543 --> 36:58.245
[SPEAKER_01]: background he was a top climber before so he was aware of risk He made his own risk assessments. He was very responsible about other people. I had

36:58.285 --> 37:08.029
[SPEAKER_01]: to persuade him to guide. Not because he didn't think he was good enough. He just thought it because he recognized the responsibility of the

37:08.129 --> 37:20.095
[SPEAKER_01]: role. So he was a very kind man and not rash and not arrogant with the skill set. He had a classic laugh. He was in different circles. He was known as

37:20.155 --> 37:31.308
[SPEAKER_01]: the crow or the vulture because Because he was tall and lean and broad-shouldered, so he had that kind of look on the ground with these

37:31.428 --> 37:41.831
[SPEAKER_01]: broad shoulders. When he was standing there, he had a narrow face, so he'd look quite quilline anyway. And then in the air, you know, he'd fly

37:41.871 --> 37:52.275
[SPEAKER_01]: like a crow. He'd do these jerky movements. He had a unique flying style. He could turn it flat in tiny bits and stronger cores. He was remarkable.

37:53.594 --> 38:01.497
[SPEAKER_01]: I always thought it was a good day. It was a special day and quite a rare day when I flew better than him. I was so competitive back then. He must have

38:01.537 --> 38:11.281
[SPEAKER_01]: found me incredibly annoying, like a crow hassling a vulture. But he was both those creatures and I was some fat, lazy seagull. So yeah, you can

38:11.321 --> 38:21.964
[SPEAKER_01]: tell from that I hold John in huge esteem. He didn't have other jobs that sustained him in paragliding. He lived on a shoestring and paraglided

38:22.004 --> 38:31.330
[SPEAKER_01]: as much as he possibly could in adventurous areas. He treated it very professionally and flew incredibly well.

38:57.736 --> 39:06.940
[SPEAKER_00]: During his time in flying, I think you must have been one of the closest persons to share the airspace with him. Did he reach any roadblocks or

39:07.100 --> 39:18.624
[SPEAKER_00]: any obstacles, be it mentally or physically, that he found difficult to overcome? And if yes, then what was his way of making the most out of any

39:18.704 --> 39:29.458
[SPEAKER_00]: adverse situation? It's quite difficult for me to answer that question for him. He was like the rest of us. He was mortal and How did he... There

39:29.478 --> 39:42.468
[SPEAKER_01]: was one great adventure flight that me and John and Jim, Mike Blubb, and who else was there? I hope I haven't forgotten anyone. We set off to fly to

39:42.488 --> 39:53.116
[SPEAKER_01]: Barabangal. Barabangal was a dream place to fly. No one had flown there. And very few even have now what they have. They've all landed up high in

39:53.156 --> 40:01.435
[SPEAKER_01]: the Barabangal Valley. We were planning on landing down by the village. We took off and we got to the watershed, dividing the Barabangal with

40:01.475 --> 40:09.978
[SPEAKER_01]: Lahadi. And on the day, it was an inverted day. One of the problems with guiding is you tend to do all the guiding in the best part of the season,

40:10.038 --> 40:15.879
[SPEAKER_01]: and then it gets more and more inverted as the season goes on. And that tends to be the time you can get out there with your mates because you're

40:15.899 --> 40:24.042
[SPEAKER_01]: not busy with your clients. Anyway, we got stymied by that and had to try and break this inversion. All of us, many FAS super experienced pilots,

40:24.748 --> 40:32.953
[SPEAKER_01]: And we were bashing around on this inversion, not managing to break through it. Maybe 50 meters below the watershed. And some of us were

40:32.993 --> 40:41.438
[SPEAKER_01]: getting up to literally four or five meters below the spine that we needed to go over the back. And I just couldn't do it. And John did. And

40:41.478 --> 40:50.243
[SPEAKER_01]: then he managed. And he was the only one of us who managed to. And he landed down over there and had a great time. And then you're also asking me how he

40:50.484 --> 40:57.429
[SPEAKER_01]: overcame physical issues. The reason why he got into paragliding in the first place. was actually because of a physical issue. He'd been

40:57.489 --> 41:06.398
[SPEAKER_01]: climbing so much. He approached climbing in much the same way as he approached paragliding. He was super determined. And as a result, he

41:06.438 --> 41:13.085
[SPEAKER_01]: had lots of falls and his ankles were in a really bad way. They were shocking to look at. They were massive. I don't know how many times he'd

41:13.125 --> 41:20.886
[SPEAKER_01]: broken his ankles. Consequently, a long walk was always a problem. And that was another way he was so good because he'd also launch really low

41:20.906 --> 41:29.567
[SPEAKER_01]: because he couldn't walk. He could walk up the top, but it was very painful for him. And he would walk up the top if he had to. But if he thought

41:29.587 --> 41:37.749
[SPEAKER_01]: there was a chance of climbing out lower down, he'd launch from lower down and climb out, which is not necessarily a recommended thing, guys

41:37.789 --> 41:44.630
[SPEAKER_01]: listening, because you don't know what the wind's going to be doing higher up. But someone with John's skill and experience often launch

41:44.670 --> 41:53.893
[SPEAKER_01]: low down and climb out. So that's how he overcame a physical ability and he was just a bit better and a bit more determined. And I think a good pilot

41:53.933 --> 42:02.240
[SPEAKER_01]: is always someone who works out his risk management strategy and flies within it. If you end up flying outside your risk management strategy,

42:02.680 --> 42:12.889
[SPEAKER_01]: even if it pays off, you're not necessarily a good pilot. You're just a lucky one because a good pilot should stay within his risk management

42:12.929 --> 42:19.916
[SPEAKER_01]: strategy. Certainly if he's a tandem pilot anyway. But he was a comp pilot, so he'd come from that comping background where it was all about

42:20.016 --> 42:29.261
[SPEAKER_01]: achieving the goal. That's a big difference for me. I didn't do that much comping. I was always taught in paragliding, be prepared to abandon the

42:29.281 --> 42:38.286
[SPEAKER_01]: goal if it's putting you at risk. That's the one I maintain, to be honest, because to me, winning is staying alive and experiencing a good life.

42:39.246 --> 42:47.189
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not necessarily winning the paragliding competition. Wise words Eddie, wise words. I think if everybody else can resonate to this that

42:47.889 --> 42:54.675
[SPEAKER_00]: winning in life is more important than winning it in a flight or in a paragliding competition. It's a very enriching way to make sure you

42:54.815 --> 43:08.323
[SPEAKER_00]: make most of not only the day but also the days to come. You have explored and seen the sport, not exactly from the beginning, but fairly early on.

43:09.203 --> 43:20.509
[SPEAKER_00]: And some of the biggest technical revolutions have happened in your lifespan. So what is next in paragliding, according to you? Oh, gosh.

43:21.911 --> 43:30.014
[SPEAKER_01]: What is next? This lightweight, small package thing, the hike and fly thing will develop a lot more. The

43:31.814 --> 43:39.637
[SPEAKER_01]: versatility of that, of being able to go for walks and enjoy it. It's interesting when I talk to my son's 22 and he's been around paragliding

43:39.677 --> 43:48.199
[SPEAKER_01]: all his life. He thinks that the younger scene is much more interesting to him and that's not comping, not cross country as such. He enjoys it.

43:49.400 --> 44:01.312
[SPEAKER_01]: He's done a bit of it. He's more the acro than the hike and fly. That's the way he sees it. Maybe some military applications, but that's probably

44:01.352 --> 44:12.835
[SPEAKER_01]: going on and we just don't know about it. I know that commercially people use them for dropping equipment, but the actual sport, I think acro and

44:12.875 --> 44:24.468
[SPEAKER_01]: hike and fly are probably the big expanding areas. The cross-country thing won't get Just more people will be doing it. More people within the

44:24.508 --> 44:35.673
[SPEAKER_01]: sport will be doing it. Whether the sport grows, that discipline itself grows, I don't know. What did the manufacturers say, incidentally? I'm

44:35.693 --> 44:44.521
[SPEAKER_01]: hoping... Most of them are just putting their word where the money is, and understandably so, because even though the sport does seem to be

44:44.561 --> 44:52.951
[SPEAKER_00]: quite expensive for the consumers, the manufacturers also complain that they don't make enough money. So this is more or less like a cash 22,

44:53.292 --> 45:03.122
[SPEAKER_00]: like, who is making money? Is it the big factories who make for different brands or is it the raw material suppliers or is it the ones who are in the

45:03.182 --> 45:12.971
[SPEAKER_00]: middle? Because consumers are definitely paying quite a premium and majority of the manufacturers are seeing a lot of good sales in the mid B

45:13.031 --> 45:22.140
[SPEAKER_00]: section, which is capable of doing fair bit of introductory accro. and does check off quite a list of boxes when it comes to chasing

45:22.160 --> 45:33.249
[SPEAKER_00]: cross-country, but at the same time is also decent for hike and fly. So I think your answer does resonate, but the only concern is that it is being

45:33.369 --> 45:41.596
[SPEAKER_00]: done on equipment which runs out quickly, like consumer ship economy where you keep on buying new stuff. every now and then, burning money,

45:41.656 --> 45:51.580
